#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096427 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "1456cdfb-6ae9-5439-8ebe-e5b41687dfa9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5337" :question/problem "What is the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1132/Source/Images/54dd85489d2ff3c834788cc817285762.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. The diagram shows that the parabola has a vertex at $(14,2)$ and passes through the point\n\n$$\n\\left(12,6\\right)\n$$\n\nThe equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^2 = 4p(x-h)\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n(h,k) = (14,2)\n$$\n\ninto the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-2)^2 = 4p(x-14)\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p$. Since the parabola passes through the point $(12,6)$, we substitute $x=12$ and $y=6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(6 - 2)^{2} &= 4p(12 - 14) \\\\\n16 &= 4p(-2) \\\\\np &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 2)^{2} &= -8(x - 14)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y= 0$. So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 2)^{2} &= -8(x - 14) \\\\\n(0 - 2)^{2} &= -8(x - 14) \\\\\n4 &= -8(x - 14) \\\\\nx - 14 &= -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nx &= -\\frac{1}{2} + 14 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{27}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{27}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096428 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddc6c4b1-b4c4-52f8-9f25-feda3130e358" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "77303537-c614-5269-a22e-0bd412e7fa99" :question/math-academy-id "e-5339" :question/problem "Find the points of intersection of the parabola $(y-2)^2=4(x+1)$ with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the points of intersection of the parabola with the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4(x + 1) \\\\\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4(0 + 1) \\\\\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4 \\\\\ny - 2 &= ± 2 \\\\\ny &= 2 ± 2 \\\\\ny &= 0, 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $y$-axis $(0,0)$ and $(0,4)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096429 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "4ce7b936-de41-5aeb-b980-80d9fa91314f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5340" :question/problem "What are the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1132/Source/Images/a0f5f271f1e4f257f3deea9c3842dcf2.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. The diagram shows that the parabola has a vertex at $(7,-4)$ and passes through the point\n\n$$\n\\left(5,-6\\right)\n$$\n\nThe equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^2=4p(x-h)\n$$\n\nand substituting\n\n$$\n(h,k) = (7,-4)\n$$\n\ninto the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+4)^2=4p(x-7)\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p$. Since the parabola passes through the point $(5,-6)$, we substitute $x=5$ and $y=-6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-6 + 4)^{2} &= 4p(5 - 7) \\\\\n4 &= 4p(-2) \\\\\np &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n(y + 4)^{2} =-2(x - 7)\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x= 0$. So, we substitute $x= 0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y + 4)^{2} &= -2(0 - 7) \\\\\n(y + 4)^{2} &= 14 \\\\\ny + 4 &= ± \\sqrt{14} \\\\\ny &= -4 ± \\sqrt{14}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $-4-\\sqrt{14}$ and $-4+\\sqrt{14}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddc6c4b1-b4c4-52f8-9f25-feda3130e358" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a5b2f28-7e99-5ecf-a292-17f279a75ec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-21,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266955} {:answer/id #uuid "613a366c-b86d-555b-aea8-9c2f39fcb1fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-12,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266956} {:answer/id #uuid "e78bcb29-5e58-5fc7-a969-cc8d4ffe0125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266957} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6c8391-2974-504a-aa15-751b8cacbd8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(21,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266958} {:answer/id #uuid "f5aa179e-eb82-5213-8c4d-ee6e8aa1106b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb6c8391-2974-504a-aa15-751b8cacbd8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(21,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266958} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bfa9bf3-3154-5936-b799-ba6ffe85b307" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266954}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4afbc9de-3c3c-55ec-9e08-58a688816d26" :question/math-academy-id "q-14185" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the parabola ${y}^{2}-x+10y+21=0$ with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "When the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x+10y+21 & =0 \\\\ (0)^{2}-x+10(0)+21 & =0 \\\\ -x+21 & =0 \\\\ x & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $x$-axis at $(21,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddc6c4b1-b4c4-52f8-9f25-feda3130e358" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a18217e9-d001-5644-9eda-1138e048a596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-54,18)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266962} {:answer/id #uuid "eb23b95d-d6e7-5692-8678-aa322bf0f8c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266963} {:answer/id #uuid "28eccccf-0e85-511d-bb72-ec24635326d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266964} {:answer/id #uuid "13d4b897-395d-57e1-acc2-0b21f187590f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266965} {:answer/id #uuid "1542c926-cc2a-51ca-80df-91da3851fc60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1542c926-cc2a-51ca-80df-91da3851fc60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266966} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba701a28-f86f-5150-94b9-bd55824337d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266961}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d20df8a-265e-56c9-b923-e820fa174518" :question/math-academy-id "q-14190" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the parabola ${y}^{2}+18x-27y+54=0$ with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "When the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}+18x-27(0)+54 & =0 \\\\ 18x+54 & =0 \\\\ 18x & =-54 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{54}{18} \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $x$-axis at $(-3,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab0feba6-4ed8-5c50-aba8-7a6a1ba9b689" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266969} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce16ccf-bf1a-5e0f-95f0-a49070129e73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266970} {:answer/id #uuid "23c483bf-8201-5984-8008-743dbbcb908d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266971} {:answer/id #uuid "fafa2fe9-c298-54ae-9840-3a425a91e6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266972} {:answer/id #uuid "bebf9dd7-d488-5833-8a26-6291e0a0e012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ce16ccf-bf1a-5e0f-95f0-a49070129e73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266970} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e088981-d698-536c-8dbb-b27115580bc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266968}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8bf318e-dc63-500a-966e-f40512cc20a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-165433" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13955472/assets/616116b9e5cf137c526b11dc5ce1647cd8f9cbd3a652be6dade9a8769467c27c.png)\n\nWhat is the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(5,15)$ and passes through the point $(2,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(5,15)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}=4p(y-15).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(2,6),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-5)^{2} & =4p(6-15) \\\\ 9 & =4p(-9) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5)^{2} & =-(y-15).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-5)^{2} & =-(y-15) \\\\ 25 & =-y+15 \\\\ y & =15-25 \\\\ y & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $-10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eac601b7-b428-57ab-a182-f986c1ddc836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266976} {:answer/id #uuid "39b2902f-7269-55ed-b64d-21a49e01c410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266977} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8b9bd4-fac1-5905-a346-2c5785dccf75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266978} {:answer/id #uuid "0d286ef6-36b7-521a-8fe1-d87f2694928a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266979} {:answer/id #uuid "c61de2ee-64f4-588a-b4d3-0aebd6c40656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e8b9bd4-fac1-5905-a346-2c5785dccf75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266978} :answer-field/id #uuid "86262259-005d-57ec-85bf-24880b46069d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266975}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20250ecc-bc9a-594d-a7f9-8fe791acfa2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-165434" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13955472/assets/6450376de8f9f4c0c8f5b414d95de67839664eda4f2056376160caddef47ba1f.png)\n\nWhat are the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(\\frac{9}{2},1)$ and passes through the point $(4,0).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(\\frac{9}{2},1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}=4p(x-\\frac{9}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(4,0),$ we substitute $x=4$ and $y=0$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-1)^{2} & =4p(4-\\frac{9}{2}) \\\\ 1 & =4p(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2}=-2(x-\\frac{9}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =(0+9) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =-2,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $-2$ and $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27e7a0d0-07af-5e4e-b493-9c279e82c57b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27e7a0d0-07af-5e4e-b493-9c279e82c57b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266983} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd19f6a9-87d8-507c-90b8-34b2f77c5a4a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266982}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "295ec8a5-8065-5b04-8ad1-001208416bc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-240666" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13955472/assets/d4c21d6ce09ff32004a28d18d223fef7858c377bccb0eb532cf2dda29a545f5e.png)\n\nThe value of the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-7,-2)$ and passes through the point $(-11,-6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-7,-2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+2)^{2}=4p(x+7).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-11,-6),$ we substitute $x=-11$ and $y=-6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6+2)^{2} & =4p(-11+7) \\\\ (-4)^{2} & =4p\\cdot (-4) \\\\ 16 & =-16p \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =-4(x+7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+2)^{2} & =-4(x+7) \\\\ 4 & =-4(x+7) \\\\ -1 & =x+7 \\\\ x & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $-8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e655a02-fa82-562a-8cb5-966692875652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e655a02-fa82-562a-8cb5-966692875652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266986} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0ed16da-cc28-5863-9b17-bcd8a2c0b6df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266985} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb8eca46-9fc7-5f1e-89e5-fa5942c1ab85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb8eca46-9fc7-5f1e-89e5-fa5942c1ab85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266988} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef1585fd-d627-5dcf-91f7-8cbfb940c537" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266987}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5d338c0-23c4-51e8-bf62-58aee9a4f5b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-251699" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13955472/assets/0da514f806c56689fdf4be724223e75c42559aa16ae38470bf088a83d299aaa9.png)\n\n**From left to right**, the values of the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-1,-8)$ and passes through the point $(5,10).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-1,-8)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(5,10),$ we substitute $x=5$ and $y=10$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5+1)^{2} & =4p(10+8) \\\\ 36 & =4p(18) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =2(y+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =2(0+8) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{16} \\\\ x & =\\pm 4-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $-5$  and $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "946e9495-5411-5a32-9d15-55fc79e6d96c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266991} {:answer/id #uuid "0c88c2c7-dac5-5150-a9a6-58bac57745c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "946e9495-5411-5a32-9d15-55fc79e6d96c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266991} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9c809e1-762d-5da3-930e-0f0f033735d7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266990} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a7abc9f-2db1-5402-8d85-39f7687436e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a7abc9f-2db1-5402-8d85-39f7687436e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266994} :answer-field/id #uuid "c38e5073-3a93-5058-ae96-d40f2f5e2a0a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266993}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1cc0f48b-955c-5fb4-87fd-77527333645f" :question/math-academy-id "q-251784" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13955472/assets/afb7abacdffafd634165fd1f35868878b2bc420df6a173b48f6a17c7638f7dec.png)\n\n**From left to right**, the **exact values** of the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-3,4)$ and passes through the point $(0,-5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-3,4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =4p(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(0,-5),$ we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+3)^{2} & =4p(-5-4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(-9) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =-(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =-(0-4) \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $-5$  and $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0a5ef85-a6ba-5f22-bbd7-e710a552ca28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0a5ef85-a6ba-5f22-bbd7-e710a552ca28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266997} :answer-field/id #uuid "35ce529b-8c25-5991-9fc9-9bc09b935ddd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266996}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4337d6a2-0dad-5b6f-9de4-86a4ea9d212e" :question/math-academy-id "q-251799" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13955472/assets/d589816d2368a450c0b2bbc1d3c6323b09b9d6dc5272f67a505b80f0688b16ae.png)\n\nThe value of the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above  is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-3,-2)$ and passes through the point $(-7,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-3,-2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}=4p(y+2).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-7,6),$ we substitute $x=-7$ and $y=6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-7+3)^{2} & =4p(6+2) \\\\ 16 & =4p(8) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =2(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+3)^{2} & =2(y+2) \\\\ 9 & =2(y+2) \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} & =y+2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{2}-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $\\frac{5}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57c30cdc-0333-55e2-a937-59c5db3cd652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57c30cdc-0333-55e2-a937-59c5db3cd652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267000} :answer-field/id #uuid "342eabd8-334e-540b-a0f0-fb6d6f7a0ccb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266999}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f600f675-ff18-55ea-865f-e9845a197e99" :question/math-academy-id "q-251810" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13955472/assets/31618ee78ebe4834cf76aedebce7c25eca8413c6a4b9d91761ccc38dbd32b01d.png)\n\nThe value of the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above  is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(2,2)$ and passes through the point $(-2,-2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(2,2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}=4p(y-2).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,-2),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=-2$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2-2)^{2} & =4p(-2-2) \\\\ 16 & =4p(-4) \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =-4(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-2)^{2} & =-4(y-2) \\\\ 4 & =-4(y-2) \\\\ -1 & =y-2 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddc6c4b1-b4c4-52f8-9f25-feda3130e358" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "884826af-ac21-5a8a-9d71-ddef3f273610" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\sqrt{\\frac{12}{7}})$ and $(0,-\\sqrt{\\frac{12}{7}})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267003} {:answer/id #uuid "8d24b636-00f0-5eda-9442-3b213eb6c4d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\sqrt{\\frac{12}{7}},0)$ and $(-\\sqrt{\\frac{12}{7}},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267004} {:answer/id #uuid "a48136ad-4893-5068-aaf4-9b3dded3396b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{12}},0)$ and $(-\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{12}},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267005} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d86e8a-ef7d-58f1-8bd2-d3b25728a28b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\sqrt{\\frac{12}{7}},0)$ and $(-\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{12}},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267006} {:answer/id #uuid "7fc7d245-609b-5b8b-b6d6-769b06cabbcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{12}},0)$ and $(-\\sqrt{\\frac{12}{7}},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d24b636-00f0-5eda-9442-3b213eb6c4d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\sqrt{\\frac{12}{7}},0)$ and $(-\\sqrt{\\frac{12}{7}},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267004} :answer-field/id #uuid "308e7392-4a45-59aa-aabb-e509c441393d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267002}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "256517c0-a227-5f0e-b206-a7c60c3cd671" :question/math-academy-id "q-73766" :question/problem "Find the points of intersection of the parabola ${x}^{2}=\\frac{3}{7}(y+4)$ with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "When the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{7}(0+4) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{12}{7} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{12}{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the intersection points are\n $(\\sqrt{\\frac{12}{7}},0)$ and $(-\\sqrt{\\frac{12}{7}},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddc6c4b1-b4c4-52f8-9f25-feda3130e358" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "781fed0e-990a-5892-859c-598e1839fb50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2\\sqrt{2})$ and $(0,-2\\sqrt{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267010} {:answer/id #uuid "742c9e6f-a9f0-59b6-9769-9438cf209dc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(5\\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(-5\\sqrt{2},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267011} {:answer/id #uuid "33e03e60-d05e-51f7-be28-8098dcc08faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2\\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,-2\\sqrt{5})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267012} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9776a0-07c8-501c-8310-9739242897d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,5\\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,-5\\sqrt{5})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267013} {:answer/id #uuid "8fa727a3-590e-5411-a9a8-df2b622cc8ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,5\\sqrt{2})$ and $(0,-5\\sqrt{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fa727a3-590e-5411-a9a8-df2b622cc8ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,5\\sqrt{2})$ and $(0,-5\\sqrt{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267014} :answer-field/id #uuid "41b57f09-ea17-534f-b5e5-e3099afb0d6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267009}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5111f4a-e172-5bc6-b474-cabc4fd38070" :question/math-academy-id "q-73767" :question/problem "Find the points of intersection of the parabola ${y}^{2}=-5(x-10)$ with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "When the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we set $x=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =-5(0-10) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =50 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{50} \\\\ y & =\\pm 5\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $y$-axis at $(0,5\\sqrt{2})$ and $(0,-5\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffb61613-6a7a-569d-ae98-a4dc317ebffc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$  and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267017} {:answer/id #uuid "10927cd1-a48a-5c2c-baac-cf2d39d3711b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267018} {:answer/id #uuid "cadabb28-2da1-5b93-8bc9-22ee579109d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267019} {:answer/id #uuid "67e04df4-c99e-5b38-b2a3-89274f048a3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267020} {:answer/id #uuid "73029ed4-5320-5eec-a240-5c35a9fd06e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $2\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67e04df4-c99e-5b38-b2a3-89274f048a3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267020} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aae2332-ed70-5fef-961b-4db1af133793" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267016}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "166cdda3-01a4-5828-9800-429450a46515" :question/math-academy-id "q-73832" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13955472/assets/e79a3a7c2fce5a3e3ec7d838ebe1327dcc5452168f499fad05eea85a08e8f8b0.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(2,-1)$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(2,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,3),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2-2)^{2} & =4p(3+1) \\\\ 16 & =4p(4) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4(0+1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $0$ and $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddc6c4b1-b4c4-52f8-9f25-feda3130e358" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "540072dd-5e36-5324-ad76-dfccfbc263f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\sqrt{6})$ and $(0,-2\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267024} {:answer/id #uuid "20aae3f4-bb26-53f8-b0c6-e7b6d0f35434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,-2\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267025} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd1790b-3e36-5763-a298-7acefc0cc9f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\sqrt{6})$ and $(0,-\\sqrt{6})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267026} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf69e79-9139-55ec-b7a7-88e4ddd26dbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no intersection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267027} {:answer/id #uuid "7036a57c-e651-5337-9702-efb1a0485a1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,-\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bf69e79-9139-55ec-b7a7-88e4ddd26dbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no intersection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267027} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c160a45-f93b-53ea-a6ea-72e629804e8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267023}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d386096-b575-518b-8d5a-59f981bff31f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73847" :question/problem "Find the points of intersection of the parabola ${y}^{2}=3(x-2)$ with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "When the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we set $x=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =3(0-2) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the equation ${y}^{2}=-6$ has no solutions, we conclude that there are no intersection points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce6dd8fc-afbf-57c9-8fab-c8122240ba64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267031} {:answer/id #uuid "0eb02928-44ea-5d32-8758-0669151e9f23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267032} {:answer/id #uuid "75fbc113-a573-54ec-8e74-b8ca43f3455b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267033} {:answer/id #uuid "69280b0f-a6e1-548b-b3d8-14fb06c58c03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267034} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c91fb6-e219-5401-9b29-aafdd6b2514c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4c91fb6-e219-5401-9b29-aafdd6b2514c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267035} :answer-field/id #uuid "92de9ed0-23fb-5ce3-a28d-bbdad3bb8222" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267030}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55a39868-2114-5c28-aa42-9882eb80ca08" :question/math-academy-id "q-79253" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13955472/assets/32d2ecb4cc0fe71888d4ca89f0c5c493d0746ae9f97a9253ef42d69f45880086.png)\n\nWhat is the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(9,4)$ and passes through the point $(-7,12).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(9,4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}=4p(x-9).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-7,12),$ we substitute $x=-7$ and $y=12$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12-4)^{2} & =4p(-7-9) \\\\ 64 & =4p\\cdot (-16) \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =-4(x-9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2} & =-4(x-9) \\\\ 16 & =-4(x-9) \\\\ -4 & =x-9 \\\\ x & =-4+9 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "826d45e3-678e-5285-a460-50fd437102d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267038} {:answer/id #uuid "d208579a-7d47-5372-9e40-973c3b6d6d1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267039} {:answer/id #uuid "e8dd48bc-6287-5249-811e-1ba720cc2d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267040} {:answer/id #uuid "525ee57b-bc73-5071-9967-ea4c8586aed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267041} {:answer/id #uuid "3a7ced0b-c506-5165-8809-3ef8ab3b5fe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "826d45e3-678e-5285-a460-50fd437102d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267038} :answer-field/id #uuid "62e7e25d-6239-522b-9cfa-1980d98e984f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267037}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3caa4df9-5e0c-5ecf-8e43-c8fb646eb03c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79294" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13955472/assets/407f89e40691ddaefd912c97dc9e8b15a08e0120ad2cf1c9a277417cd8380e98.png)\n\nWhat is the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(4,-4)$ and passes through the point $(7,5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(4,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}=4p(y+4).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(7,5),$ we substitute $x=7$ and $y=5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(7-4)^{2} & =4p(5+4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2} & =y+4 \\\\ 16 & =y+4 \\\\ y & =16-4 \\\\ y & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $12.$"}]}
